course: dictionary
lesson: 13342
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is right. «We caught a hackney outside the station.» You catch a taxi, not a horse, so here a hackney is a taxi. Context decides.
explain: Look for the clue words. «The hackney trotted past, lifting its knees high.» Trotting and lifting knees are things horses do, so this hackney is a horse. Ask yourself: what fits in this place in the sentence?
explain: Some words are also names. Hackney is the name of an area of East London, and a capital letter is a clue. «We walked through Hackney to visit my cousin.» Check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence: does it still make sense?
fact: A word can have more than one meaning.
fact: «Hackney» means a hackney carriage: the official name for a taxi in the UK, especially a black cab.
fact: «Hackney» can also mean a breed of horse or pony with a high-stepping trot, once used to pull carriages.
fact: «Hackneyed» means used so often that it has become boring, as in «a hackneyed phrase».
fact: Hackney is also the name of a borough (area) in East London.
exercise: «We caught a hackney outside the station.» What does hackney mean here? | An area of London | A horse with a high-stepping trot | *A taxi | A boring phrase
exercise: «Her story began with a hackneyed phrase, «once upon a time», and everyone yawned.» What does hackneyed mean? | Brand new and exciting | Written in pencil | Very old and valuable | *Used so often that it has become boring
exercise: Nadia says hackney is always a horse. Is she right? | Yes, it is always a taxi | *No, it depends on the sentence | Yes, it is always a horse
test: In «The hackney trotted past, lifting its knees high», which word is the clue that it is a horse? | The word The | *The word trotted | The word past | The word high
test: What does Hackney mean in «We walked through Hackney, a busy borough»? | A taxi | *An area of East London | A boring phrase | A horse
exam: In which sentence does hackney mean a taxi? | *«We caught a hackney outside the station.» | «Her phrase was hackneyed.» | «We walked through Hackney.» | «The hackney stepped slowly along the lane.»
